#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "648e9e50-03bf-56ba-a4ad-caf142c9628a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14574" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2-2x + 3}\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx^2-2x + 3\n& = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ (1^2 - 1^2) + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x^2 - 2 \\cdot x+ 1^2) - 1 + 3 \\[3pt]\n& = (x-1)^2+2\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n=\n\\arctan u + C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-1)/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{2}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "77952d82-6a9b-5a6b-921e-32cbe82ea298" :question/math-academy-id "e-14575" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4x - 4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-4{x}^{2} & =-(4{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\  & =-((2x)^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+1-1) \\\\  & =-((2x-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(2x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x-1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x - 1).$  \n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu =2x - 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac12\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x-1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "4e0188f0-4d2b-5f3c-b1bd-12270e3adc0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14576" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    x^2-2x&= x^2 -2x +(1-1)\\[3pt]\n              &= (x^2 -2x +1)-1\\[3pt]\n         &= (x-1)^2-1\n         \n  }\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n= \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-1.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-1.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3} \\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{x^2-2x}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=  \\int_{1+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{3}\\dfrac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{(x-1)^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\dfrac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=  \\textrm{arcsec}\\, {u}\\Big|_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\[5pt]\n&= \\textrm{arcsec} \\left(2\\right)  - \\textrm{arcsec}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "4b6820ef-32a8-5d26-84f6-a464db90129a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14577" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{36 -(3x - 2)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36(1-(3x-2)^{2}/36)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}/36}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{3x-2}{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((3x-2)/6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "975fa508-bc91-5946-bd33-73148d6865ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{4}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065489} {:answer/id #uuid "e713076d-a815-50dc-9bba-50df0b48aebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065490} {:answer/id #uuid "f16bd10c-a0ba-51ec-8204-16f27b3adf1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065491} {:answer/id #uuid "610040b2-a83f-5888-9b50-41614b5e709a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (2x - 3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065492} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea0c985-2f21-52c3-b3fa-e0da19d227bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (2x - 3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e713076d-a815-50dc-9bba-50df0b48aebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (\\frac{2x - 3}{2}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065490} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f0771a-0095-5b48-b0c3-545dd56e20f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "772ac28e-e690-51b7-a882-989bee35e089" :question/math-academy-id "q-114906" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4((2x-3)^{2}/4+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x-3)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{2x-3}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-3}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((2x-3)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x-3}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc4e2844-9405-5fed-a29f-dab9b853f6b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287796} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a7b86-a616-564b-b503-9ca68157feb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287797} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8eab86-4d58-553c-9802-19c20e12297e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287798} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1843d0-e852-5c0f-95ee-8071b6b71345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287799} {:answer/id #uuid "55d03b51-bcaa-5e9f-8ed8-6db0a96fdf74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8eab86-4d58-553c-9802-19c20e12297e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287798} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8bed8b0-61d4-5251-8dbf-7d8696065cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6681a8c-31b6-5f48-9d3b-97034642ec2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114910" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+18 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+18 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+18 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-3}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-3}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "734090e9-27a6-5fbc-82dd-c3d1a0dff657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287803} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2bc090-ba43-5d12-ba31-888a29ae9e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287804} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf97aba-2ded-5e96-bd6f-cdd8fa187770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287805} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f1c9e6-1a7e-5d36-abdd-50f0f4371849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287806} {:answer/id #uuid "7f558a44-33cb-57aa-84bd-2db1dc9d066f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f558a44-33cb-57aa-84bd-2db1dc9d066f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287807} :answer-field/id #uuid "e735d61e-420d-5df2-b60c-63df9371b943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5659f7b5-791c-542b-a3d9-13c7b7bc51e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114918" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{(6x+1)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-(6x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4(1-(6x+1)^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x+1)^{2}/4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x+1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{6x+1}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{6x+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-(6x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x+1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x+1}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "586b0dde-511e-5bb5-9c37-772f0e035ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287810} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddd3e6c-cb17-5c93-8b6a-a47a8348b4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(2(x-3))+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287811} {:answer/id #uuid "07a1c9e4-d47f-5d7b-b286-d0842049db8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287812} {:answer/id #uuid "382d42e4-ae69-5cba-aafe-ecc70e4de169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287813} {:answer/id #uuid "eed3ebc9-7f7d-5a37-a91b-1327f1fc849e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586b0dde-511e-5bb5-9c37-772f0e035ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287810} :answer-field/id #uuid "17373a13-7b16-5704-962f-669fd03c644c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "873e2926-85ca-5ec7-abc8-aaeead8b9483" :question/math-academy-id "q-114923" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x-3|\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d822e8-df7c-50e0-b8b4-b41079ddb825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287817} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cd1339-cdf6-5e40-a452-fda68c4390fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287818} {:answer/id #uuid "83759bb4-35c2-5617-8baf-d53388173b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287819} {:answer/id #uuid "368f2d7e-ee9b-521f-8b8d-329f658f5aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287820} {:answer/id #uuid "18478743-6d72-5aa3-8ba5-f1fb882e0b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "368f2d7e-ee9b-521f-8b8d-329f658f5aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287820} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe87f52-1903-58ce-966a-ffa328dbd257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "221832ca-e386-5d28-a4c3-7b381df79beb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114926" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x-7).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=2x-7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a51075ec-18ad-5576-8319-4b595a19798c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287824} {:answer/id #uuid "affa2fa1-b65a-5547-94e5-ebe1c340d507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825} {:answer/id #uuid "2be6aa11-5099-5871-a5e4-0a1127e30e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287826} {:answer/id #uuid "dddc2260-7f03-530e-a23b-29f44c68d2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287827} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eff953-de69-5a86-8d3c-aeda99affaeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "affa2fa1-b65a-5547-94e5-ebe1c340d507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac18518-2867-5216-8a0c-5524d038b46b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecf9beb7-988d-5771-b00a-91910c2e65e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114928" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+15 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-8x+16)-16+15 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{(x-4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-4.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{(x-4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a6a02c8-3b4c-5a99-98b0-0f18b251a08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831} {:answer/id #uuid "0c87793f-9b74-52d8-9a3a-2c1313823f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287832} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb06adb-d268-5637-8dd8-4924aeb0ae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287833} {:answer/id #uuid "9bab1b50-1b32-578e-ba20-924ef4f520cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287834} {:answer/id #uuid "ce922632-a3c9-5efd-ba8c-332ae6262352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a6a02c8-3b4c-5a99-98b0-0f18b251a08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ef12ff-d148-51b2-924b-8a963408fabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e78637ee-142d-5498-8358-000fe537dac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19527" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+10 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+10 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+10 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{5}{(x-3)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-3.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $3+\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =5(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e134e2-af87-5230-82e7-d3fabe832e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287838} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1e2f24-c802-5d61-9e90-3f0ed160528a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287839} {:answer/id #uuid "3c76e52c-53b7-5820-99ca-19e8c345706b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287840} {:answer/id #uuid "c18839b2-829e-5943-a79d-cf78c138840f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287841} {:answer/id #uuid "528e7523-cb33-59eb-bf79-dcbb4c2e886f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e134e2-af87-5230-82e7-d3fabe832e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287838} :answer-field/id #uuid "435600ce-1f95-513f-94a5-ca9860e2f862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db8ef128-2717-5091-8870-85aa35ff95e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19532" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-12x+36)-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1577cfd-1e25-5cb7-9d6a-87540a7fd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066155} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2dbf11-17b5-555a-8e31-2c202a10a3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066156} {:answer/id #uuid "24cf40d4-b719-5709-a125-8b18ffff6f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066157} {:answer/id #uuid "3d407f86-c506-5087-b4ac-1638a8651a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066158} {:answer/id #uuid "7482f316-63e5-5578-8283-9a99334dec41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24cf40d4-b719-5709-a125-8b18ffff6f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066157} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1978f3e-8e8a-5f95-b2af-a7840684e23f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8f3f828-4a9b-54dd-a6d3-6f4468d7c192" :question/math-academy-id "q-238056" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-13/2$ | $-11/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec8b8aaf-d204-5921-b8dc-46aa10be26f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287845} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4d6847-2ccf-59b0-8def-c166b0ac6c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287846} {:answer/id #uuid "a2461bfe-66cf-50fe-a011-0cbc5ec1fb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287847} {:answer/id #uuid "44cc03d4-d63e-5d74-b3c1-ccafcf64985c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287848} {:answer/id #uuid "a5635b89-a1ab-5a2b-9999-1f49cad0e3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec8b8aaf-d204-5921-b8dc-46aa10be26f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287845} :answer-field/id #uuid "556d78ea-74f4-5e0e-850e-f8b58048555d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "badf1a99-06f8-53a3-a425-75f049c0400e" :question/math-academy-id "q-270103" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12-x(x+12) & =-12-({x}^{2}+12x) \\\\  & =-12-({x}^{2}+12x+36-36) \\\\  & =-12-((x+6)^{2}-36) \\\\  & =24-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24(1-(x+6)^{2}/24)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{24}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}/(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+6)/2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+6}{2\\sqrt{6}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+6}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-6-\\sqrt{6}$ | $-6+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+6)/2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}u. \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =15(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fdebd8b-bdc1-5e45-80a5-9a3f66c963d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287852} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b6d904-b1b7-5c8d-9e99-ebffdc67a8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287853} {:answer/id #uuid "51331f52-9825-5234-b4f0-96c70ab174eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287854} {:answer/id #uuid "e11312a4-c456-51e5-8a30-68446cc09a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287855} {:answer/id #uuid "ab49e003-e88e-51d6-93f1-472fcdd040d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e11312a4-c456-51e5-8a30-68446cc09a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287855} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b15f1f-3ed7-58a8-bf44-c7b7b48bff3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4624471-57ca-5285-8ea0-5ae498ff1110" :question/math-academy-id "q-270105" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x-3 & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+[{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}]+3) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+2.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-5/2$ | $-3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c14e7f4-9b4f-5294-b193-a55437cab9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c14e7f4-9b4f-5294-b193-a55437cab9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287859} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39d2d0a-d133-5783-8bbb-1d1a172af46b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6ab7859-3821-57d4-a2e8-b5a880e14da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-348530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+3 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+3 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+3 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-2+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c19f438e-258d-58c6-adaa-6d0fb1e160d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c19f438e-258d-58c6-adaa-6d0fb1e160d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287862} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8f1a62-3343-5afe-8f7b-9bcd3b339e7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f6f7fe5-e124-5c20-b6f5-4662a4de938e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348533" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+15 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+15 \\\\  & =(x+4)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=5{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{1}{|x+4|\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-4+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =5(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d8ff3d-494f-562c-a6a9-a22b56975923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d8ff3d-494f-562c-a6a9-a22b56975923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287865} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e99c19-2e78-50ef-87a1-91d7d4d4005e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75621e3-8feb-5c5d-968c-e25e78e132b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-348547" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2x & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+[{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}]) \\\\  & =-((x+1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =arcsin\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db6e4cf3-803a-5942-9ce0-7047942e078a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsine Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c53bc774-3c81-5053-b670-6f0a6b38fa06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287868} {:answer/id #uuid "13927e05-86d6-5555-aa8f-9d072e2d31b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c53bc774-3c81-5053-b670-6f0a6b38fa06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287868} :answer-field/id #uuid "885a7a1b-e581-5da6-b923-85adbeef9fdc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8b055b7-b338-5782-a064-5a2d7bd3c0f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-348550" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-18x-80 & =-({x}^{2}+18x+80) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 9x+[{9}^{2}-{9}^{2}]+80) \\\\  & =-((x+9)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+9)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+9)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+9).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{-8}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-18x-80}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7671c97c-2afb-50f1-8a52-8a5fdd530304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7671c97c-2afb-50f1-8a52-8a5fdd530304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287872} :answer-field/id #uuid "6018a901-83d4-586a-be85-47dd39a7b727" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49bc5d77-ad19-594f-a0c9-4b98d2248f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348554" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+5 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+5 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x-2)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fbe6fe7-068e-5354-8619-a9249365a932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:answer/id #uuid "5962bdf2-38a4-5364-bdcd-ce8c421c1bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5962bdf2-38a4-5364-bdcd-ce8c421c1bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} :answer-field/id #uuid "03211610-2eaf-5d3c-a0c7-b3bfd62133db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "300779b2-6c1e-5908-a102-8f06afbf164c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348570" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{4-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{5}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+17 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+17 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+17 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{(x-4)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{5}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/\\sqrt{3}}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-arctan\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a04d97-3a55-5e8f-9aaf-283c1cdb5feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287879} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d42fb-c0d8-5f9d-9b1a-00771ab15317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287880} {:answer/id #uuid "23dd957e-6734-5a93-b8e0-c7889c95d246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287881} {:answer/id #uuid "75244f30-917f-55be-a4b3-7232ca8913af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cot}^{-1}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287882} {:answer/id #uuid "9106dc59-2daa-5719-ad25-9a4451fc5d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9106dc59-2daa-5719-ad25-9a4451fc5d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287883} :answer-field/id #uuid "8111a402-de5b-5641-baca-fa89eca50b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e3ad59e-5530-5f96-9f4b-8c2f8b5873d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-3588" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+20 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+20 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+20 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-4}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30687a7e-c5ab-59fb-80b0-15e4948aa5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287886} {:answer/id #uuid "289c0919-0600-5792-958a-2c3b563c7a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287887} {:answer/id #uuid "632a160d-7ab1-5c53-9a1a-b9d01c4b6bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+5)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287888} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9b1745-abfc-52ba-8666-a256bb52fd0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+1}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287889} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab97437-ccd1-51d8-95d9-8664bd307b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+5}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "289c0919-0600-5792-958a-2c3b563c7a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287887} :answer-field/id #uuid "282b3c49-9d67-5f6e-87e8-505bda1b70a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "589ac1f9-7e27-5253-a295-86acfab0073f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49689" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-25 & =25[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}-1] \\\\  & =25[{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}-1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{25[((x+2)/5)^{2}-1]}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot 5\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{15}{|x+2|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\frac{x+2}{5}.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f228e6-97ae-5ca3-926c-072d0498b747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287893} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1bf09e-7b3c-5923-a001-88f2b54347e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287894} {:answer/id #uuid "69aaae0a-82af-5900-a2b2-ce958cd96ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287895} {:answer/id #uuid "7f191337-b974-5575-a73d-cf7e29d01e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896} {:answer/id #uuid "47674c73-88a4-502c-88df-65e644a99c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f191337-b974-5575-a73d-cf7e29d01e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287896} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21707fa-6d7c-5098-9f16-ff9ba2374d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd859552-b72d-56d1-8bd5-a359ca7b8880" :question/math-academy-id "q-80312" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+1).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x+1|\\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
